C++ Base Pointer Without Virtual Function
Description

This C++ program demonstrates static binding using a base-class pointer and a derived-class object.

The Base class contains a display() function, and the Derived class defines its own display() function. However, since the display() function in the base class is not virtual, the base-class version is called when accessed through a base-class pointer.

Classes Used
1. Base Class

The Base class is the base class.

It contains the display() function.
The display() function displays "Base display function".
The function is not declared as virtual.
2. Derived Class

The Derived class inherits from the Base class.

It defines its own display() function.
This function displays "Derived display function".
Main Function

In the main() function, a Derived object is created.

A base-class pointer is then made to point to the derived object:

Base* basePointer = &derivedObject;

The display() function is called using the base pointer:

basePointer->display();

Since display() is not virtual, the Base class function is called.

Output
Base display function
OOP Concept Used

Static Binding / Base-Class Pointer Without Virtual Function

        Base
         |
     display()
         ↑
         |
      Derived
         |
     display()
         |
         ↓
 Base pointer
         |
         ↓
Base display function
Important Point

Because the function is not virtual:

void display() const

the compiler binds the function call to the Base class version.

If the function were declared virtual, it would demonstrate run-time polymorphism instead.